use std
into video_check.amni

Imports: os, math and gfx.video. A standalone program that checks the H.264/MP4 video encoder on the CPU and prints one line per check, then "video: ok" when every check passed or "video: FAILED" otherwise. These already exist in namespace video and must not be defined here: `video::start(w, h, fps, qp, gop)` gives a bool; `video::add(rgba)` takes a read-only byte slice of width times height times 4 bytes and gives a bool; `video::save(path)` writes the .mp4 and gives a bool; `video::error()` gives the last reason as text; `video::keep_recon` is a module bool and `video::recon` a growable byte list that collects every frame's decoded-equivalent picture as planar YUV 4:2:0 (Y, then U, then V); `video::frame_bytes(i)` gives frame i's coded size; `video::psnr_y()` gives the last frame's luma PSNR in dB as a real. A module bool `failed` starts false; `check(name, ok, detail)` prints "PASS " or "FAIL " then the name, then " (" the detail ")" when the detail is not empty, and sets `failed` when ok is false. Time is always read as `os::clock_ns()` with the namespace written out. The work folder is the entry at index 1 of `args()` when there are at least 2 entries (index 0 is the program name), otherwise "hc/test/gfx/out"; it is created with `os::make_dir` when missing.

Test pictures: `paint(kind, t, w, h, px)` fills the growable byte list px (cleared first) with a w by h RGBA picture for frame t, alpha 255, using only whole-number arithmetic. Kind 0 still: red 255 times x divided by w, green 255 times y divided by h, blue (x plus 2 y) modulo 256 divided by 2 plus 64. Kind 1 pan: with u equal to x plus 3 t and v equal to y plus 200 minus 2 t, red (u times 4) modulo 256, green (v times 3) modulo 256, blue 230 when (u divided by 8 plus v divided by 8) is even, else 30. Kind 2 zoom: with dx equal to x minus w/2 and dy equal to y minus h/2 and r equal to 6 plus 3 t, inside the circle (dx squared plus dy squared below r squared) red 250, green (40 plus 20 t) modulo 256, blue 40; outside red 20, green (x times 255 divided by w), blue (y times 255 divided by h). Kind 3 noise: every channel the next value of a 32-bit xorshift generator (x becomes x exclusive-or (x shifted left by 13), then exclusive-or (x shifted right by 17), then exclusive-or (x shifted left by 5), each kept to 32 bits) seeded with 2463534242 plus t, taken modulo 256. Kind 4 cut: kind 1 for frames below 5, else kind 0 with red and blue swapped. Kind 5 extremes: 255 for every channel when (x plus y plus t) modulo 4 is below 2, else 0.

The cases, each a name, kind, width, height, frame count, qp and gop: "still 64x48" 0 64 48 7 26 100; "pan 96x64" 1 96 64 10 26 100; "pan crop 50x30" 1 50 30 8 20 4; "pan intra 34x18" 1 34 18 6 10 1; "zoom 80x80" 2 80 80 8 32 100; "noise 48x32" 3 48 32 5 26 100; "noise q51 48x32" 3 48 32 4 51 2; "cut 64x64" 4 64 64 10 26 100; "extremes 32x32" 5 32 32 6 10 100; "tiny 2x2" 1 2 2 3 26 100; "pan 176x144 q40" 1 176 144 12 40 100; "extremes q51 16x16" 5 16 16 3 51 100.

`ffmpeg_same(path, w, h, frames)`: runs with `os::run_command` the text: "ffmpeg -nostdin -v error -i " path " -f rawvideo -pix_fmt yuv420p -y " path ".yuv 2> " path ".err"; it gives true only when the command gives 0, the .err file reads and is empty, and the .yuv file reads and equals `video::recon` byte for byte (its length must be frames times (w times h plus 2 times (w/2) times (h/2))). Otherwise it fills a module text `last` with what differed ("ffmpeg exit", "ffmpeg said: " and the error text up to 200 bytes, "length", or "byte " and the first differing index).

V0: "ffmpeg found": `os::run_command("ffmpeg -version > /dev/null 2>&1")` gives 0; when it does not, V1 and V4 are skipped (the check fails).

V1: every case, one check line per case named "V1 " and the case name: `video::keep_recon` set and `video::recon` emptied, `start`, then `paint` and `add` for every frame (summing `psnr_y()` over the frames), `save` to the work folder as "video_" plus the case number plus ".mp4", then `ffmpeg_same`. The detail is the file size in bytes, " bytes, " and the average luma PSNR with one decimal and " dB", or `last` or `video::error()` on failure.

V2: quality and size. "V2 qp 10 quality": the average luma PSNR of "pan intra 34x18" and of "extremes 32x32" are both above 44 dB. "V2 qp 26 quality": the average of "still 64x48" and of "pan 96x64" both above 36 dB. "V2 still frames are cheap": in "still 64x48" every frame after the first is coded in under a tenth of the first frame's bytes. The PSNRs and frame sizes come from V1's runs (keep them in module lists).

V3: bad input is refused: `start` gives false for width 0, width 33, height 9000, fps 0 and gop 0; after a good `start(32, 32, 30, 26, 10)`, `add` gives false for a slice of 100 bytes; after another good start with no frames, `save` gives false. One check line with the number of refusals seen out of 7.

V4: speed. 30 frames of kind 1 at 1280 by 720, qp 26, gop 30, fps 30, `keep_recon` set, timing only the `add` calls with `os::clock_ns()`; saved as "video_speed.mp4" and checked with `ffmpeg_same`. The detail is the frames per second with one decimal and the file size in kilobytes; it passes when `ffmpeg_same` passes and the speed is above 10 frames per second.

`main` runs V0, then V1, V2 and V4 when ffmpeg is found, V3 always, and finally prints "video: ok" or "video: FAILED".
